window.COACH = {"page": "data-physicalization", "title": "Data Physicalization", "prizeName": "Education, Integrated Human Practices", "summary": {"verdict": "Your page has evidence most outreach pages lack: a knowledge question with a right answer, counts for every option, and a question about the map itself. What holds it back is repetition and facts that do not match. The map and sound exhibit are described three or four times, the survey is presented as being about this booth when it covered the whole event, and several details disagree with each other or with the IHP and Geospatial pages.", "strengths": ["Every survey result has a count, a base and a percentage, and the knowledge question has a right answer (32 of 62 picked Yilan).", "You report a weak result honestly and turn it into a design target: the LED flashing was hard to read.", "A real dialogue moment: visitors expected Taipei and asked why the data showed otherwise.", "Clear science writing: the cited definition of data physicalization and the one-line explanation of cavitation.", "Reusable detail in places: the data-to-element table, the listening website, and the headphones wired to laptops behind the poster."], "priorities": ["Say once, in the abstract and at the start of the Impact section, that the survey covered all six booths, and mark which results are about the map. Then match the labels and categories to the IHP page.", "Rewrite the abstract so its first sentence names both exhibits and the event, every ledger line is a full sentence, and the Taipei surprise appears as your dialogue example.", "Fix the facts that disagree: Yilan alone or Yilan and Nantou, the tourism explanation, “easy to see” against 32 of 62, the Figure 3 labels and caption, and the “most memorable” translation.", "Describe each physicalization once. Move the build before the results, and cut 1.4, the repeated concept paragraphs and the repeats in the conclusion.", "Name the data (crop, measure, years, source), the exact LED and color rules and the pitch shift, and add a build list so another team could make the board.", "Close the loop: say what you changed or will change because only half read the map, and report the Instagram event with dates and numbers."], "ballot": [{"question": "How well did their work promote mutual learning and/or a dialogue?", "status": "partial", "where": "3. Stress map: Public interaction; 2.1 (the design target)", "note": "Visitors’ surprise about Taipei and the half-right map reading are real dialogue, but the page never says what you changed because of them."}, {"question": "Is it documented in a way that others can build upon?", "status": "partial", "where": "3. Mapping data to physical elements; Sound of plants: Physicalization method (listening site)", "note": "The encoding table and listening site help, but there is no named data source, LED rule, materials list, code or pitch-shift setting."}, {"question": "Was it thoughtfully implemented?", "status": "partial", "where": "2. Impact; 1.3 The data", "note": "The right-answer question is well designed, but the page credits the booth with results from a whole-event survey and never says when or where the booth ran."}, {"question": "Did the team convince you that their activities would enable more people to shape, contribute to, and/or participate in synthetic biology?", "status": "missing", "where": "4. Conclusion (Instagram event)", "note": "Synthetic biology appears only in a conclusion claim, and the Instagram event has no dates or numbers."}], "skeleton": {"releaf": ["1. Overview (goals, why physicalize, the data, from data to map)", "2. Impact (knowledge, attitude, practice, summary)", "3. Physicalizations (stress map, sound of plants)", "4. Conclusion", "References"], "winners": ["Purpose: why public learning matters, in one or two paragraphs", "Structure statement: how the page is organised", "Groups of activities, each opening with why this audience", "Per activity: setting and headcount, what was taught, the hands-on part, how participants engaged, a measured or quoted outcome, what the team changed, materials to reuse", "Forward look: who continues it and where the materials live"], "gap": "Your page reports impact before it explains how the map works, and it explains the map in both 1.4 and section 3. Winners keep each activity’s setting, build, engagement and outcome together, so open with the event, then each physicalization with its build and what visitors did, then the survey, then what you will change."}, "extras": ["Section 2.2: the line “95.2% of participants agreed our Data Physicalization Map helped them understand the relationship between climate change and crop yield.” repeats the sentence just above it. Cut it, or keep it and cut the number from the paragraph.", "Sound of plants method cards: “Khait, Hadany et al.” but References: “Khait, I., Lewin-Epstein, O., Sharon, R., et al.” Use one form everywhere, e.g. “Khait et al. (2023) [1]”.", "Sound of plants feedback: “some even mentioning how the sound physicalization was the most memorable part” should be “some even said the sound physicalization was the most memorable part”.", "Conclusion: “Additionally according to our surveys, many people” needs commas: “Additionally, according to our surveys, many people”.", "Section 2.1: “Yilan’s high stress amount was showcased through its frequent flashing of LED lights.” Write: “Yilan’s high stress was shown by its fast-flashing LEDs.”", "Figure 2 caption: “Showcasing knowledge of the region with the highest stress.” does not say what was asked. Write: “Figure 2. Answers to ‘Which region has the highest plant stress?’ (n = 62). The correct answer, Yilan, is green.”", "Figure 4: the chart shows “Selected at least one future sustainable action: 98 percent”, but the text never gives this number. Add it, e.g. “61 of 62 (98.4%) chose at least one action” (61 is the only count out of 62 that rounds to 98%).", "Sound of plants concept: “how impactful it would be if the public realizes how plants can also sense stress” mixes tenses and overclaims. Write: “how much it would mean if the public learned that plants respond to stress”.", "Event name: the page says “public forum booth” (Overview), “booth at our public forum” (Figure 1) and “public engagement booth” (Sound of plants). Your IHP page treats the morning forum and the afternoon booths as separate parts. Use one name, e.g. “our booth at the Water Garden Organic Farmers’ Market”.", "Abstract ledger: the heading “Results” sits over two rows (“Stress map”, “Sound of plants”) that are exhibits, not results. Rename it, e.g. “What we built and found”.", "Abstract section map: “1 Overview · From data to map” names only the last subsection. Write: “1 Overview · goals, the data, and how we built the map”.", "Abstract section map: “2 Impact · Connecting data to plant stress” names only 2.1. Write: “2 Impact · what 62 visitors knew, felt and planned to do”."]}, "live": "https://2026.igem.wiki/gems-taiwan/", "mockPages": ["human-practices", "education", "entrepreneurship", "sustainability", "laws-and-regulations", "geospatial-analysis", "data-physicalization"], "wiki": "4b52192, 2026-10-08", "notes": [{"id": "dp-a01", "zone": "abstract", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "We developed a data physicalization map that showcases different stress levels and crop yields throughout Taiwan.", "title": "Lead with both exhibits and the event", "body": "This is the first sentence a judge reads, and it names only the map. It leaves out the sound station, the booth and who came. Say in one sentence what visitors could do and where.", "after": "At our booth on 5 September 2026, we turned plant-stress data into a map of Taiwan that visitors could see and plant recordings they could hear.", "excerpts": ["edu-2025-og-heidelberg-01", "gis-2024-og-marburg-01"], "excerpt_note": "Both openings say what was done and why in the first sentence or two, before any detail."}, {"id": "dp-a02", "zone": "abstract", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "We designed a physical map using styrofoam, colored paper, and LED lights", "title": "Swap materials for what the map shows", "body": "The paragraph is four lines copied from different sections, and this one spends the summary on materials. A judge needs what the colors and lights mean, and one moment of dialogue. Your best one is in the body: visitors expected Taipei to show the most stress.", "after": "Paper color shows crop yield and flashing LEDs show plant stress. Many visitors expected Taipei to show the most stress, and asked why [Yilan] did.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "How well did their work promote mutual learning and/or a dialogue?"}, {"id": "dp-a03", "zone": "abstract", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "We had 62 respondents and a total of 116 people who visited our booth.", "title": "The survey covered the whole event", "body": "The 62 answers come from one survey that visitors filled in after all six booths (IHP page, 5.2), so the sheet should not tie them to this booth alone. The ledger row for the same numbers is tagged “Forum”, but your IHP page treats the morning forum (“more than 50 participants”) and the afternoon booths as separate parts. Give visitors first, then respondents.", "after": "Of the [116] people who visited our booth, 62 answered the event survey, which covered all six booths.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Was it thoughtfully implemented?"}, {"id": "dp-a04", "zone": "abstract", "type": "praise", "anchor": "59 of 62 participants (95.2%) agreed", "title": "The right number to put in bold", "body": "This is the one survey question that asks only about the map, and you give the count and the base with the percentage. It is the number a judge should remember from this page.", "excerpts": ["ihp-2025-ug-nyuad-03"], "excerpt_note": "NYU gives the sample size, the count and the percentage together.", "ballot": "Was it thoughtfully implemented?"}, {"id": "dp-a05", "zone": "abstract", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "read the map correctly on a single, brief encounter", "title": "Say what “correctly” means", "body": "Putting your weak result in bold is honest, and judges trust a page that does it. On its own, though, “read the map correctly” does not say what visitors had to do. Name the task and the count, and add what you will change.", "after": "Just over half of visitors (32 of 62) found the highest-stress region from the LEDs alone, so we will [change to the board].", "excerpts": ["edu-2025-og-heidelberg-06"], "excerpt_note": "Heidelberg states a weak result plainly and says what it took from it.", "ballot": "How well did their work promote mutual learning and/or a dialogue?"}, {"id": "dp-a06", "zone": "abstract", "type": "praise", "anchor": "More frequent flashing meant more severe stress", "title": "A line that works on its own", "body": "Someone who has not read the page understands this line: it gives the rule for reading the LEDs in seven words. The other ledger lines should do the same.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dp-a07", "zone": "abstract", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "recordings of drought-stressed tomato and tobacco", "title": "Finish the sound line", "body": "This is a fragment with no verb, so a reader does not know what visitors did. The clicks are ultrasonic, so also say they were pitch-shifted down; otherwise a judge will wonder how anyone heard them.", "after": "Visitors heard drought-stressed tomato and tobacco, pitch-shifted down from ultrasound", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dp-a08", "zone": "abstract", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "32 of 62 respondents (51.6%) correctly identified", "title": "Correctly identified what?", "body": "The line stops before its object. Marking this row “Partial” is honest; finish the sentence so it makes sense on its own.", "after": "32 of 62 (51.6%) picked Yilan as the highest-stress region", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dp-a09", "zone": "abstract", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "60 of 62 participants (96.8%) said they were willing", "title": "Willing to do what?", "body": "The line is cut off before the action. Say what they were willing to do, and keep in mind this question was about the whole event.", "after": "60 of 62 (96.8%) would share what they learned with family or friends", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dp-001", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "The picture shows our map physicalization being explained at our public forum booth.", "title": "Open with what, where and when", "body": "Your overview starts by describing a photo, so a judge does not yet know what the booth was, where it stood or who came. Open with the event, the two physicalizations and your numbers (the date and place below are from your IHP page). Also fix the grammar: “The different colors ... represents” should be “represent”.", "after": "On 5 September 2026, at the Taipei Water Garden Organic Farmers’ Market, our booth showed plant stress in two ways: a map of Taiwan you could see and plant recordings you could hear. On the map, paper color shows crop yield, from dark green (highest) to brown (lowest). Red LEDs flash faster where plant stress is higher. [116] people visited the booth and 62 answered our survey.", "excerpts": ["edu-2025-og-heidelberg-01", "gis-2024-og-marburg-01"], "excerpt_note": "Both openings tell the reader what was done and why in the first few lines, before any detail.", "ballot": "Was it thoughtfully implemented?"}, {"id": "dp-002", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "As climate change intensifies, stress is often invisible to the general public.", "title": "Say whose stress you mean", "body": "On a page called “Stress data you can touch”, a reader may first think of human stress. Say plant stress, and name the kind (heat, drought). “To really feel the gravity of climate warnings” is also vague; say what people cannot see.", "after": "Climate change is putting more heat and drought stress on Taiwan’s crops, but people rarely see it until the harvest drops. We built a map of Taiwan that visitors can see and interact with, so the link between climate, plant stress and crop yield is easier to follow than on a graph.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dp-003", "type": "praise", "anchor": "is a physical artifact whose geometry or material properties encode data", "title": "A cited definition before the term", "body": "You define data physicalization with a quoted, cited sentence before you use the term again. A judge from another field knows exactly what you mean from the first line of the section. Wageningen, a 2025 winner, explains a trade term the same way.", "excerpts": ["ihp-2025-og-wur-03"], "excerpt_note": "Look at how “binders” is explained in the same sentence where it first appears."}, {"id": "dp-004", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "These visualizations help convey the data in a more accessible way (Jansen).", "title": "Jansen is missing from your references", "body": "You cite Jansen twice in 1.2, but the References list only has Khait et al. Add the full Jansen entry. This definition is usually traced to Jansen et al. (2015), “Opportunities and challenges for data physicalization”, CHI 2015; check that it is the source you used. The last sentence of 1.2 only repeats the definition, so you can cut it.", "after": "- Jansen, Y., et al. ([year]). [Title]. [Where published]. [doi or link].", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dp-005", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Our selected datasets focus on crop yield and heat stress, making the data comparable across locations.", "title": "Name the datasets, crop and years", "body": "Section 1.3 explains how you chose data but never names it. Your IHP page says the map showed “heat stress and rice crop yields”, and Figure 4 on the Geospatial page uses nighttime temperature and rice yield for 2013 to 2016. If that is the board’s data, say so here. A judge cannot read a map without knowing the crop, the measure and the years.", "after": "The board uses two county-level datasets: rice yield from [source, years] and heat stress, measured as [nighttime temperature], from [source, years]. We chose heat because it is one of the stresses ReLeaf protects against, and yield because it shows what that stress costs farmers.", "excerpts": ["gis-2024-og-marburg-04", "gis-2024-og-marburg-17"], "excerpt_note": "Marburg says what the data had to do, then names the dataset and tools in two sentences.", "ballot": "Is it documented in a way that others can build upon?"}, {"id": "dp-006", "type": "cut", "anchor": "The process of first acquiring the data, identifying its pattern, and turning it into a visual representation", "title": "This sentence repeats the two before", "body": "This sentence says again what the two sentences before it said, in more words. Cut it. Also cut the opening “Rather than selecting data solely because it was readily available” unless you name a dataset you turned down.", "after": "We first found the patterns in the data that matter most for plant stress, then chose a physical feature of the map to show each one.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dp-007", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "We designed a physical map using styrofoam, colored paper, and LED lights", "title": "Show the build before the results", "body": "Section 2 reports how visitors read the map, but the build is explained in section 3. Section 1.4 and section 3 also describe the same map and sound exhibit almost word for word. A clearer order is Overview, Physicalizations (how you built each), Impact (what visitors learned), Conclusion. Then 1.4 shrinks to two lines.", "after": "We built two physicalizations: a stress map of Taiwan and a plant-sound listening station. Section 2 explains how we made each one, and section 3 shows what visitors learned from them.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Was it thoughtfully implemented?"}, {"id": "dp-008", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "many do not realize that plants can actually feel the impact of stress", "title": "Plants do not “feel” stress here", "body": "The paper you cite shows that drought makes water columns in the stem snap (cavitation), and that this makes a sound. It does not show that plants feel anything. “Feel” here, and “sense stress inflicted upon them” in the Sound of plants section, claim more than the research shows. Say that plants respond to stress, and that some responses make sound.", "after": "Plants respond to stress in ways people cannot see. Few people know that a plant under drought stress makes sounds.", "excerpts": ["ihp-2023-og-sdu-03"], "excerpt_note": "SDU limits its claim to exactly what was tested."}, {"id": "dp-009", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "After they visited our booth, we asked for them to fill out a form", "title": "Say the survey covered the whole event", "body": "This line says the form measured how much of “our booth” visitors understood. Your IHP page says visitors filled in one survey after collecting stamps at all six booths, and your Sound of plants section says visitors gave feedback “on the overall event”. Some answers (coffee, the ReLeaf device) came from other booths. Say this once here so judges know which results belong to the map. Also, “them” has no person to refer to.", "after": "At the end of the event, visitors who had been to all six booths filled in one survey. 62 of the [116] people who visited our booth answered it. [Two] questions were about the map itself; the rest covered the whole event.", "excerpts": ["ihp-2025-ug-nyuad-03"], "excerpt_note": "NYU says who was surveyed and how many before giving any percentage.", "ballot": "Was it thoughtfully implemented?"}, {"id": "dp-010", "type": "praise", "anchor": "32 of 62 respondents (51.6%) correctly identified Yilan County, the region we represented as highest-stress.", "title": "A real test, with every answer counted", "body": "You asked a question with a right answer, and you give the count, the base and the percentage for every option, including the wrong ones (32 + 12 + 11 + 7 = 62). That is measured knowledge. Heidelberg, a 2025 winner, only measured self-rated knowledge and gave no number of participants.", "excerpts": ["ihp-2025-ug-nyuad-03", "edu-2025-og-heidelberg-05"], "excerpt_note": "NYU gives sample size, count and percentage together, as you do. Heidelberg shows how a result reads without an n.", "ballot": "Was it thoughtfully implemented?"}, {"id": "dp-011", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Just over half of visitors read the map correctly on a single, brief encounter", "title": "Honest about a weak result", "body": "You say plainly that only about half read the map correctly, and you turn it into a design target. Judges trust your strong numbers more when the weak one is on the page too. One tip: this is one 54-word sentence, so split it in two at “but”.", "excerpts": ["edu-2025-og-heidelberg-06"], "excerpt_note": "Heidelberg admits its pub quiz missed its audience and says what it took from that.", "ballot": "How well did their work promote mutual learning and/or a dialogue?"}, {"id": "dp-012", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "making the LED flashing-to-stress-level mapping more immediately legible", "title": "Say what you will change", "body": "You name the problem (the flash speed is hard to read) but not the fix. Close the loop with one concrete change and how you would check it. This answers the ballot’s mutual learning question: visitors’ answers changed your design.", "after": "Next time, we will put a legend beside the board that shows [three] flash speeds and what each one means. We will then check whether more than [x]% of visitors pick the right region.", "excerpts": ["edu-2024-og-aachen-02", "edu-2025-og-heidelberg-08"], "excerpt_note": "Each passage goes from what participants said to what the team changed, in two or three sentences.", "ballot": "How well did their work promote mutual learning and/or a dialogue?"}, {"id": "dp-013", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "the impact of climate change on Taiwan's coffee and crop production", "title": "Same survey item, different labels", "body": "The text gives three answers for this item: “very much understood” (39), “partially” (21) and not well (2). Figure 3 shows the same item as agree, neutral and disagree, and its caption says “impact of stress”, though the question asks about climate change. Your IHP page puts the same 62.9% under Knowledge (high and moderate understanding); this page puts it under Attitude. The question also names coffee, which came from the coffee booth. Pick one label set and one category and use them on both pages.", "excerpts": ["edu-2024-og-aachen-06"], "excerpt_note": "Counter-example: even a winner slipped here. Aachen gives two different view counts on one page."}, {"id": "dp-014", "type": "praise", "anchor": "The physicalization itself was directly credited for this: 59 of 62 participants (95.2%) agreed or strongly agreed", "title": "A question about the map itself", "body": "This is the strongest evidence on the page: one question asks only whether the map helped, and you report the full spread, including the 2 neutral and 1 disagree answers. One small change: “for this” ties it to the result above, which other booths also shaped. Write “Visitors credited the map directly:”.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Was it thoughtfully implemented?"}, {"id": "dp-015", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "several participants specifically pointed to the plant stress sounds rather than the map or spoken explanation", "title": "Count the “several” and quote one", "body": "“Several participants” is vague when you have 62 written answers. Give the number who named the sounds, and quote one; your Sound of plants table already has good ones. The last sentence (“doing real cognitive work, not just serving as ambience”) claims more than a few comments can show, so cut it.", "after": "When we asked what was most memorable, [n] of 62 visitors named the plant sounds. One wrote “知道植物缺少水分的時候居然會發出聲音” (“I was surprised to learn plants make sounds when they lack water”). For these visitors, hearing the clicks made plant stress real.", "excerpts": ["edu-2025-og-heidelberg-03"], "excerpt_note": "A participant quote that states what she thought before and after, in her own words.", "ballot": "How well did their work promote mutual learning and/or a dialogue?"}, {"id": "dp-016", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "Together, these results trace a clear data of their understanding, awareness, and likelihood of future actions.", "title": "End on what the data shows", "body": "“Trace a clear data of” is not correct English. The next sentence (“left not just informed, but reporting an intention to change their own behavior”) also sounds stronger than a survey of intentions can support. Say what each part of the survey showed, and that these are plans.", "after": "Together, the three parts of the survey show what visitors understood, how their views changed, and what they said they would do. Most said they would share what they learned and buy local or sustainably farmed food. [Say whether you followed up to see if they did.]", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dp-017", "type": "cut", "anchor": "Our data physicalization was inspired by the idea that scientific data should not only be seen, but also experienced.", "title": "This repeats your Goals section", "body": "“Invisible”, “tangible” and “accessible” all appeared in 1.1 Goals, so these two paragraphs mostly say it again. Keep the one new idea, the link to ReLeaf, and cut the rest.", "after": "Plant stress starts inside the plant, where people cannot see it. Our booth made it visible and audible. This mirrors the core idea of ReLeaf: connecting hidden biological signals with information people can understand and act on.", "excerpts": ["edu-2025-ug-queens-05"], "excerpt_note": "Counter-example: even a winner made the same point twice in one paragraph. Cutting the second half lost nothing."}, {"id": "dp-018", "type": "cut", "anchor": "We created the physical map using styrofoam as the base material", "title": "Already said in 1.4", "body": "This paragraph, and the sound paragraph below it, repeat section 1.4 almost word for word. Keep the full version here, with the color list and the table, and cut 1.4 to two lines. The last sentence of the LED paragraph also repeats the sentence before it.", "after": "We built the map on a styrofoam base. Colored paper shows crop yield, and LEDs show plant stress.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dp-019", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "The frequency or amount of LED flashing increased with the severity of stress", "title": "Frequency or amount: which one?", "body": "“The frequency or amount of LED flashing” could mean faster flashing or more LEDs, and “intermediate colors” does not say which colors or how many yield bands. A reader, or a team copying your board, needs the exact rule. Also say what pattern visitors should notice: what color is Yilan, the county that flashes fastest?", "after": "Each county has [n] red LEDs. They flash at one of [three] speeds: [speed] for low stress, [speed] for medium and [speed] for high. The paper comes in [four] colors, from dark green ([yield range, unit]) through pale yellow-green to brown ([yield range, unit]).", "excerpts": ["gis-2022-ug-iiser-pune-india-04"], "excerpt_note": "IISER reads one legend color aloud and says what it stands for.", "ballot": "Is it documented in a way that others can build upon?"}, {"id": "dp-020", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "We mapped each type of data to a physical or sensory element", "title": "Add a build list others can copy", "body": "Your table is a good encoding key. To answer the ballot question on documentation others can build on, add what another team would need: board size, number of LEDs, the controller and code, the data file, the cost and the build time.", "after": "Materials: styrofoam board ([size]), colored paper ([n] shades), [n] red LEDs, [controller] running [code link], [power source]. Cost: [amount]. Build time: [hours]. Data file: [link].", "excerpts": ["edu-2024-ug-cityu-04", "edu-2023-og-epfl-02"], "excerpt_note": "CityU lists materials and steps for the next organiser; EPFL says exactly what its repository contains.", "ballot": "Is it documented in a way that others can build upon?"}, {"id": "dp-021", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Many believed that regions such as Taipei would have the most stress instead.", "title": "A real moment of dialogue", "body": "This is the best dialogue evidence on the page: visitors expected Taipei, your data showed otherwise, and they asked why. Heidelberg won Best Education with the same move: a belief the public held, what the team showed, and what followed. Finish it by saying what you learned from their surprise.", "excerpts": ["edu-2025-og-heidelberg-04"], "excerpt_note": "One specific belief, what the team showed, and what the team concluded.", "ballot": "How well did their work promote mutual learning and/or a dialogue?"}, {"id": "dp-022", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "We explained the tourism in Nantou and Yilan were affecting its stress level.", "title": "Yilan alone, or Yilan and Nantou?", "body": "Your survey marks Yilan as the one highest-stress region, but this paragraph says “Yilan and Nantou had the highest stress”. The reason you gave visitors, tourism, is in none of the data on this page, and the Geospatial Analysis page links Yilan’s risk to warm nights in the rice belt. Check which counties the board showed as highest and what your data says causes it. Grammar: “curious on why” should be “curious about why”, and “the tourism ... were affecting its” should be “tourism ... was affecting their”.", "after": "Many visitors expected Taipei to show the most stress and were surprised that [Yilan] did. When they asked why, we explained that [reason from your data, e.g. warm nights during the rice season]. Their surprise showed us that people tend to link stress with big cities, so we [what you changed or will change].", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dp-023", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "The feedback we got that addressed the stress map was the fact that areas that receive stress were easy to see", "title": "Easy to see? Your survey says half", "body": "Here the high-stress areas were “easy to see”, but section 2.1 says only 32 of 62 picked Yilan and that the flashing should be “more immediately legible”. Both can be true, but say so. “Teach the Taiwanese public more about their own country” also claims more than one quote can show; the table below has one comment about the map.", "after": "Some visitors told us the flashing made high-stress areas easy to spot, as in the comment below. Our survey shows this worked for about half of them: 32 of 62 picked Yilan. A legend would help the rest.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dp-024", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "Plants respond to stress in ways that are often invisible to the human eye until visible damage has already occurred", "title": "Split this long paragraph, in time order", "body": "This paragraph has five long sentences that mix the science, the idea for the booth and the link to ReLeaf. The next paragraph then tells how you found the paper, which happened first. Put events in order: the paper you found, what it showed, what you built. Use short sentences, and cite [1] where you write “recent research”.", "after": "When we read Khait et al. (2023) [1], we learned that plants under drought stress make ultrasonic clicks, far above human hearing. We wanted visitors to hear them. The map let people see plant stress, and the recordings let them hear it. This matches ReLeaf’s core idea: stress can be detected before it turns into damage you can see.", "excerpts": ["ihp-2023-ug-hust-03"], "excerpt_note": "HUST explains a specialist idea in plain words before it uses it."}, {"id": "dp-025", "type": "praise", "anchor": "plants experience a physical process called cavitation, where continuous water columns inside the plant snap and form air bubbles", "title": "A hard term explained in one line", "body": "You name the process and explain it in the same sentence with an image anyone can picture: water columns snap and form air bubbles. A judge from any field can follow it. HUST and Wageningen, both prize winners, explain specialist terms this way.", "excerpts": ["ihp-2023-ug-hust-03", "ihp-2025-og-wur-03"], "excerpt_note": "Both explain the term at first use, in plain words, before building on it."}, {"id": "dp-026", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "we first collected plant sound recordings from scientists who had studied and recorded plants under different stress conditions", "title": "Say where the sounds came from", "body": "“Collected ... from scientists” sounds as if you contacted the researchers, but your References say the recordings come from Dryad, an open data archive, under a CC0 licence. Say that. Also say how visitors could hear them: Figure 6 puts the clicks at about 40 to 80 kHz, far above human hearing, and your listening site says they were pitch-shifted down. A judge will wonder how ultrasound came out of headphones.", "after": "We used the open recordings from Khait et al. (2023), shared on Dryad under a CC0 licence [1]. The clicks are ultrasonic, so we shifted their pitch down by [factor] into the range people can hear. We built a website to play them, and at the booth two headphones were connected to laptops hidden behind the poster.", "excerpts": ["gis-2024-og-marburg-17"], "excerpt_note": "Marburg names its data source and tools in two sentences.", "ballot": "Is it documented in a way that others can build upon?"}, {"id": "dp-027", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "Most people had inquiries about how plants were able to make these noises in which we explained", "title": "Question, answer, reaction, in order", "body": "The first sentences are hard to follow (“had inquiries about how ... in which we explained”). The explanation here (“water would be hard to suck up”) also differs from the cavitation explanation above. Use one explanation in plain words, and end on what visitors said.", "after": "Many visitors were surprised that plants make noise under stress, and most asked how. We explained that a dry plant pulls hard on the water in its stem, and the water column can snap and form an air bubble. Each snap makes a pop, a bit like bubble wrap. [One thing a visitor said or asked next.]", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "How well did their work promote mutual learning and/or a dialogue?"}, {"id": "dp-028", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Hearing the crop's stress sound was the most memorable part", "title": "The translation says more than the original", "body": "聽到作物的壓力聲音印象深刻 means that hearing the crop’s stress sound “left a deep impression”. It does not say “most memorable”. Section 2.2 builds on this answer, so keep the translation exact. Showing the Chinese next to the English is a good idea; just check each line. The line below also needs a subject: “I learned that plants actually make sounds when facing stress or danger.”", "after": "Hearing the crop’s stress sound left a deep impression on me.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "dp-029", "type": "cut", "anchor": "Our data physicalization booth helped us achieve our goal of making plant stress and synthetic biology more accessible", "title": "Cut the repeats and the synthetic biology claim", "body": "The conclusion repeats the sound example and the survey from earlier, and “dire situation” is stronger than your data. It also says the booth made synthetic biology more accessible, but nothing on this page teaches synthetic biology. Either add one line on what visitors learned about it, or drop the claim.", "after": "Our booth turned plant-stress data into something visitors could see and hear. In our survey, 32 of 62 found the highest-stress region on the map, and 59 said the map helped them understand the link between climate and crop yield. Next, we will [change to the board].", "excerpts": ["edu-2023-og-epfl-04"], "excerpt_note": "Counter-example: even a winner closed on a general claim a reader cannot check. End on a result or a next step."}, {"id": "dp-030", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "we began promoting a plant stress event where participants upload a picture of a stressed plant", "title": "Give the Instagram event dates and results", "body": "“We began promoting” does not say when, how many people took part, or whether it is still running. A judge asking whether your work lets more people take part needs those facts. Report numbers first, the way Heidelberg opens each section, or write it as a plan with a date.", "after": "From [date] to [date], we ran a plant stress photo event on Instagram: people posted a photo of a stressed plant from their daily life and said why they chose it. [n] people took part, from [places].", "excerpts": ["edu-2025-og-heidelberg-02"], "excerpt_note": "Three one-line results, each with a number or a format, before the story.", "ballot": "Did the team convince you that their activities would enable more people to shape, contribute to, and/or participate in synthetic biology?"}], "excerpts": {"edu-2023-og-epfl-02": {"id": "edu-2023-og-epfl-02", "prize": "education", "team": "EPFL", "year": 2023, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/epfl/education", "section": "BioQuest", "function": "loop-closing", "text": "The Git repository contains the .godot file, all the files required to build the game, and a README guide that explains how to build and run the game using GODOT.\nThe README also offers insights into game design, code, and instructions on how to utilize the template effectively. This resource not only enables gameplay but also facilitates easy modifications for future IGEM teams. You can also access the PDF of the readme below for your convenience.", "why_it_works": "Says exactly what is in the repository (.godot file, build files, README) and what the README covers, so a future team knows what it will get before opening the link.", "teaches": ["reusability"], "prizeName": "Education"}, "edu-2023-og-epfl-04": {"id": "edu-2023-og-epfl-04", "prize": "education", "team": "EPFL", "year": 2023, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/epfl/education", "section": "Open days-booth", "function": "conclusion", "text": "The positive response from the visitors to this weekend of discovery reaffirmed the importance of science outreach and education. It showcased the value of making complex concepts accessible to any audience, sparking interest, and potentially shaping the future of STEM education and research.", "why_it_works": "Counter-example: 25,000 visitors came to the open days, but the paragraph closes on 'positive response' and 'reaffirmed the importance of science outreach'. Say how many people reached the booth and one thing they asked.", "teaches": ["evidence", "concision"], "prizeName": "Education"}, "edu-2024-og-aachen-02": {"id": "edu-2024-og-aachen-02", "prize": "education", "team": "Aachen", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/aachen/education", "section": "Rolling Out AlphaFold Decoded", "function": "loop-closing", "text": "As we received enthusiastic responses at international iGEM meetups, we recognized the need for a more flexible learning option, which led us to adopt hybrid workshops. However, even in these workshops, many participants expressed a desire for an asynchronous format, as fixed timelines often clashed with their academic schedules or individual pacing. This feedback pushed us to develop a more comprehensive solution, resulting in a nine-part video series on YouTube. This format allows learners worldwide to engage with the material at their own pace, ensuring that no one is left behind regardless of their other commitments.", "why_it_works": "The clearest feedback-to-change chain in the casebook: offline workshops, then hybrid ones, then participants asked for asynchronous learning, so the team made a nine-part video series. Each step names its reason.", "teaches": ["loop-closing", "two-way-dialogue"], "prizeName": "Education"}, "edu-2024-og-aachen-06": {"id": "edu-2024-og-aachen-06", "prize": "education", "team": "Aachen", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/aachen/education", "section": "Reception and Impact", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "So, this is the content – it’s certainly comprehensive and covers a lot of ground. Despite the complexity and depth of the topic, we were genuinely excited to see that our videos reached a much wider audience than expected, gathering an impressive 2,920 views. This level of engagement shows there’s a real interest in diving deep into such challenging material. Even more rewarding were the comments we received from viewers, sharing their thoughts, questions, and appreciation. Here’s a look at some of the feedback that’s helped shape and motivate our journey.", "why_it_works": "Counter-example: the page header says 1,567 views on YouTube, this paragraph says 2,920. Every number that appears twice on a page must match, or say which date each refers to.", "teaches": ["consistency", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Education"}, "edu-2024-ug-cityu-04": {"id": "edu-2024-ug-cityu-04", "prize": "education", "team": "CityU-HongKong", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/cityu-hongkong/education", "section": "2. Synthetic Biology Activity Handbook", "function": "loop-closing", "text": "To extend the target readers of our handbook, we translated it into multiple languages, including Russian, Korean, Indonesian, Simplified Chinese, and Traditional Chinese. We also provided a list of materials and detailed procedures for the activities to lessen the burden on event organizers. By easing the preparation for organizing the synthetic biology workshop, we hope students from diverse age groups get interested in learning synthetic biology.", "why_it_works": "Explains why the handbook is reusable in practical terms: five translations, a materials list, and step-by-step procedures aimed at organisers. The paragraph before it says the handbook came from noticing that such workshops are rare locally.", "teaches": ["reusability", "inclusion"], "prizeName": "Education"}, "edu-2025-og-heidelberg-01": {"id": "edu-2025-og-heidelberg-01", "prize": "education", "team": "Heidelberg", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/heidelberg/education", "section": "Educating Today’s Researchers to Think Translational", "function": "page-opening", "text": "We help young scientists navigate Heidelberg’s innovation ecosystem to learn how to bridge the gap between fundamental research and patient impact. Our panel discussion opened up mutual dialog between students and leading figures in scientific translation, patient advocacy, regulatory affairs, and clinical research, showcasing current challenges and opportunities. A regional guide connects students with local translational support networks and offers a clear overview of programs. Promoting social dialog, we organized a pub quiz, a workshop booth in the old town and succeeded in the hei_INNOVATION Ideas Competition, addressing societal concerns and contributing to the public understanding of synthetic biology.", "why_it_works": "A short abstract for the first half of the page: one sentence of purpose, then one clause per activity. A judge who reads only this paragraph knows every activity in the subgoal.", "teaches": ["structure", "concision"], "prizeName": "Education"}, "edu-2025-og-heidelberg-02": {"id": "edu-2025-og-heidelberg-02", "prize": "education", "team": "Heidelberg", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/heidelberg/education", "section": "Panel Discussion - Ethics and Regulation", "function": "section-intro", "text": "- We hosted a discussion on cell and gene therapy ethics and regulation, reaching 140 attendees as well as the public on YouTube\n- We created a platform for dialog between students and an interdisciplinary panel of experts\n- We inspired young scientists to expand their skills beyond the lab towards clinical translation", "why_it_works": "Three one-line results before the story: a number (140 attendees), the format, and the intended learning. Each section of the page opens this way, so readers can skim the page by its bullets.", "teaches": ["structure", "summary-first"], "prizeName": "Education"}, "edu-2025-og-heidelberg-03": {"id": "edu-2025-og-heidelberg-03", "prize": "education", "team": "Heidelberg", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/heidelberg/education", "section": "Feedback from the Audiences", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "“As a Molecular Biotechnology student, the discussion had a big impact on me. I hadn’t focused much on regulation, ethics, and translation before, but I realized how interesting and crucial these areas are for my future as a researcher. The speakers were thoughtfully chosen and reflected a strong diversity of perspectives.” - Clara, student participant", "why_it_works": "A participant quote that states a before and after in her own words ('I hadn’t focused much on... but I realized'). The team introduces it with 'Rather than describing how great it was, we would like to share how our audience described its impact'.", "teaches": ["evidence", "learning-outcome"], "prizeName": "Education"}, "edu-2025-og-heidelberg-04": {"id": "edu-2025-og-heidelberg-04", "prize": "education", "team": "Heidelberg", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/heidelberg/education", "section": "Workshop Booth in Heidelberg's Old Town", "function": "reflection", "text": "An example of a particularly hotly debated topic was whether mRNA vaccines can integrate into the genome. People of all ages were convinced that this was possible, with many even claiming to have read something about it, a dangerous misconception, often fueled by misinformation. When we showed images illustrating the separation of the mRNA vaccine and the host’s DNA, the participants were visibly convinced and relieved. Surprised, we noticed that even a topic as prominent as COVID vaccines was seemingly not communicated clearly enough to the public. This motivated us to talk to as many people as possible.", "why_it_works": "Shows dialogue going both ways: the public held a specific misconception, the team showed a specific image, and the team learned something about public communication that changed its plan. One concrete example beats a paragraph of claims.", "teaches": ["reflection", "two-way-dialogue", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Education"}, "edu-2025-og-heidelberg-05": {"id": "edu-2025-og-heidelberg-05", "prize": "education", "team": "Heidelberg", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/heidelberg/education", "section": "Workshop Booth in Heidelberg's Old Town", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "To measure our impact, we asked participants to mark their interest and self-assessed knowledge on a graph before and after the game. After analyzing the results, we found that interest increased by 20% and self-assessed knowledge grew by 56.3%. This shows that, with the right approach, the general public is interested in and willing to engage with science.", "why_it_works": "States the method in one sentence (mark interest and knowledge before and after) and the result in the next. Caveats for students copying it: the page gives no number of participants, and the figure caption says an unpaired test was used on before/after data.", "teaches": ["evidence", "method"], "prizeName": "Education"}, "edu-2025-og-heidelberg-06": {"id": "edu-2025-og-heidelberg-06", "prize": "education", "team": "Heidelberg", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/heidelberg/education", "section": "Science Pub Quiz", "function": "limitation", "text": "Overall the event received excellent feedback, particularly from international students who appreciated the event being conducted in English, as such opportunities are limited in Heidelberg. While the event aimed to reach the general public, we noticed the audience primarily consisted of natural science students. This made us realize the importance for additional efforts to engage an even broader range of people outside the science bubble.", "why_it_works": "Admits the event missed its target audience and says what the team took from that. Naming a limit in plain words makes the positive claims around it more believable.", "teaches": ["reflection", "limitation"], "prizeName": "Education"}, "edu-2025-og-heidelberg-08": {"id": "edu-2025-og-heidelberg-08", "prize": "education", "team": "Heidelberg", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/heidelberg/education", "section": "Interactive Learning Platform", "function": "loop-closing", "text": "We acquired feedback from students from the Summer School and School workshops to test and refine the platform. As a result, we decided to implement our \"Wetlab Series\", instructional videos that demonstrate key molecular lab techniques and contribute to the variety of available content, making our platform more interactive and appealing.", "why_it_works": "Two sentences, one loop: whose feedback (Summer School and workshop students), and what changed (a new video series). Short and checkable.", "teaches": ["loop-closing", "concision"], "prizeName": "Education"}, "edu-2025-ug-queens-05": {"id": "edu-2025-ug-queens-05", "prize": "education", "team": "Queens-Canada", "year": 2025, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/queens-canada/education", "section": "Queen's Healthcare & Business Conference", "function": "audience-framing", "text": "Many attendees, while familiar with healthcare and business concepts, have limited experience translating scientific knowledge into practical solutions or entrepreneurial models. This gap can hinder the ability to see biotechnology not only as a scientific discipline but also as a field with tangible commercial and societal impact. The competition also provided an opportunity to expose participants from business or non-STEM backgrounds to biotechnology, sparking interest and encouraging them to consider how science and innovation intersect with business and societal needs. While QHBC attendees are often familiar with healthcare and business topics, many have limited exposure to the cutting-edge research and technological innovations driving biotechnology. This gap can limit understanding of how emerging scientific discoveries translate into real-world applications and ultimately shape the future of healthcare altogether. This opportunity also engaged participants from non-STEM backgrounds, helping to spark interest in biotechnology and showing how science can intersect with business and greater societal needs.", "why_it_works": "Counter-example: the paragraph makes its point twice. Sentences 1 to 3 and 4 to 6 repeat the same two ideas (limited exposure to biotech, non-STEM participants). Cutting the second half loses nothing.", "teaches": ["concision", "repetition"], "prizeName": "Education"}, "gis-2022-ug-iiser-pune-india-04": {"id": "gis-2022-ug-iiser-pune-india-04", "prize": "gis", "team": "IISER-Pune-India", "year": 2022, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2022.igem.wiki/iiser-pune-india/model", "section": "Climate Modelling for Waterlogging hotspots in India", "function": "definition", "text": "The dark green parts are the NDVI masking layer where anything below 0.20 NDVI has been masked. Basically, this masking was done to exclude the water bodies from the NDMI-masked areas.", "why_it_works": "Reads one legend colour aloud and says why that layer exists. Every map caption could do this.", "teaches": ["clarity", "assumed-knowledge"], "prizeName": "GIS page"}, "gis-2024-og-marburg-01": {"id": "gis-2024-og-marburg-01", "prize": "gis", "team": "Marburg", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/marburg/gis", "section": "Abstract", "function": "page-opening", "text": "The global demand for natural rubber is reshaping the landscape of agricultural cultivation, driving expansion into new regions and increasing environmental risks. Using high-resolution geospatial analysis, we mapped the global distribution of Hevea brasiliensis (natural rubber) cultivation under current and future climate scenarios, generating the most detailed model to date. This analysis highlights shifts in suitable cultivation zones driven by climate change and identifies key ecological challenges, including the potential spread of the devastating South American Leaf Blight (SALB).", "why_it_works": "Problem, method, result and the risk found, in four sentences. A reader who stops here knows what was mapped, at what scale and why it matters.", "teaches": ["structure", "concision"], "prizeName": "GIS page"}, "gis-2024-og-marburg-04": {"id": "gis-2024-og-marburg-04", "prize": "gis", "team": "Marburg", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/marburg/gis", "section": "Data & Technical Background", "function": "method", "text": "To achieve our objective of analyzing rubber cultivation under current and future climatic conditions, identifying a suitable dataset was essential. Based on previously published classifications of cultivation zones, we determined that the dataset needed to include monthly measurements of precipitation and temperature. One of the problems we identified while reviewing similar models was their coarse spatial resolutions, which limited predictive accuracy for detailed analyses and prompted us to seek higher-resolution datasets.", "why_it_works": "Sets the requirements for the data before naming any dataset, and says what was wrong with earlier models. The later choice then reads as a consequence.", "teaches": ["structure", "clarity"], "prizeName": "GIS page"}, "gis-2024-og-marburg-17": {"id": "gis-2024-og-marburg-17", "prize": "gis", "team": "Marburg", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/marburg/gis", "section": "Materials and Methods", "function": "method", "text": "We utilized the CHELSA V2.1 dataset for climate data and the Global Administrative Areas (GADM) dataset for geographic boundaries. Geospatial visualization were performed using QGIS (version 3.36.3). Data processing and analysis were conducted in Python (version 3.11.6) using the libraries fiona (version 1.9.6), rasterio (version 1.3.10) and numpy (version 1.26.2).", "why_it_works": "Datasets, software and library versions in two sentences. Short, complete enough to rebuild the environment.", "teaches": ["method", "concision"], "prizeName": "GIS page"}, "ihp-2023-og-sdu-03": {"id": "ihp-2023-og-sdu-03", "prize": "ihp", "team": "SDU-Denmark", "year": 2023, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/sdu-denmark/human-practices", "section": "Avoid greenwashing", "function": "limitation", "text": "Since the vitroZymes currently have only been tested in their ability to degrade the molecule PFOA, we cannot allow water work companies to promote our product as a complete solution to removing PFAS from the environment as of right now. We need to underscore that vitroZymes only have the potential to degrade other molecules, but further investigation is required.", "why_it_works": "Limits the product claim to what was tested (PFOA only) and says what partners may not say about it. A rare passage that guards the page's claims against overreach.", "teaches": ["consistency", "limitation", "evidence"], "prizeName": "IHP"}, "ihp-2023-ug-hust-03": {"id": "ihp-2023-ug-hust-03", "prize": "ihp", "team": "HUST-China", "year": 2023, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/hust-china/human-practices", "section": "5.1 Initial Hardware Design For Coal-Fired Power Plants", "function": "definition", "text": "During our discussions with Professor Li, he explained that the heat produced by industrial facilities can be categorized into high-quality heat that can be efficiently utilized and low-quality heat that cannot. Many existing applications predominantly make use of high-quality heat while neglecting the potential utility of low-quality heat, whereas our specific need for a cultivation temperature of 30℃ aligns with the potential utilization of low-quality heat. Professor Li suggested that we explore the use of low-quality waste heat from coal-fired power plants, such as waste heat from cooling towers, to maintain the required temperature for our microbial cultivation. This suggestion provided valuable inspiration for our hardware design.", "why_it_works": "Explains a specialist idea (high- vs low-quality heat) in plain words before using it, then shows why it fits the 30 ℃ culture need. The reader is never asked to know thermal engineering.", "teaches": ["assumed-knowledge", "clarity", "loop-closing"], "prizeName": "IHP"}, "ihp-2025-og-wur-03": {"id": "ihp-2025-og-wur-03", "prize": "ihp", "team": "WageningenUR", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/wageningenur/human-practices/index.html", "section": "#3 Choice of the platform material for the coating and EU regulations", "function": "definition", "text": "Traditionally, seed coatings have relied on petroleum-based binders (adhesives, or “seed stickers”). This is a practice soon to be outlawed, as we found out from our stakeholders in the seed sector. Our consultation with Amalia Kafka from Euroseeds highlighted the urgency of this change, due to the upcoming EU microplastics restrictions under the EU chemical legislation REACH:\n- By 2028: deliberately added microplastics will be restricted in agricultural products, including seed treatments.\n- By 2031: the restriction will extend to all plant protection products and treated seeds.", "why_it_works": "Glosses the trade term in the same sentence ('binders (adhesives, or “seed stickers”)'), names the source, and turns the regulation into two dated bullets a reader can check.", "teaches": ["assumed-knowledge", "evidence", "clarity"], "prizeName": "IHP"}, "ihp-2025-ug-nyuad-03": {"id": "ihp-2025-ug-nyuad-03", "prize": "ihp", "team": "NYU-Abu-Dhabi", "year": 2025, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/nyu-abu-dhabi/human-practices", "section": "Campus CDG Awareness Survey", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "To understand awareness levels in our own community, we conducted a campus survey of 97 participants. Only 9 (9.2%) had heard of CDG before, quantifying the education gap we needed to address. However, over 80% expressed interest in learning more about rare diseases. When asked about preferred learning formats, 25% requested infographics and posters, approximately 35% preferred short video content, and over 40% requested on-campus events and workshops—insights that directly shaped our educational outreach approach.", "why_it_works": "Gives the sample size, the raw count and the percentage, then ties each format preference to the outreach that followed. Every number is there to justify a decision.", "teaches": ["evidence", "loop-closing", "concision"], "prizeName": "IHP"}}};
